# 7. IMPORTANT — PROTECT INITIAL RENDERING & PERFORMANCE

Performance is a priority.

The existing content must not become hidden indefinitely when scripts fail or JavaScript is unavailable.

## 7.1 Progressive Enhancement

The default page state must remain visible.

Only activate hidden/reveal animation states after the animation system has successfully initialized and confirmed that animations are allowed.

If:

- JavaScript is unavailable.
- IntersectionObserver is unavailable.
- The animation controller fails.
- The page is already being restored from browser history.
- Reduced motion is enabled.

Then:

**All page content must remain immediately visible without animation.**

Never make `opacity: 0` the permanent default for critical content.

Provide a safe fallback state.

## 7.2 LCP Protection

The main LCP element must not be unnecessarily delayed.

Important:

- Do not delay image fetching.
- Do not delay image decoding.
- Do not delay SSR content.
- Do not add artificial rendering barriers.
- Do not introduce global loading overlays.
- Do not make the primary hero image depend on a late animation initialization.

The primary hero image should remain visible promptly.

Its appearance may be visually refined, but its rendering must not be delayed by the animation engine.

## 7.3 CLS Protection

Animations must not change document layout.

Use transforms and opacity.

Avoid changing the final position or dimensions of page elements.

Ensure that images maintain explicit aspect ratios or dimensions.

## 7.4 Interaction Availability

The following elements must never wait for entrance animations to finish before becoming usable:

- Product purchase buttons.
- Search input.
- Filters.
- Navigation.
- Cart controls.
- Checkout inputs.
- Quantity selectors.
- Variant selectors.
- Form controls.
- Dialog triggers.

Animation is visual only.

